   "author": "Hollowmere team",
   "story": "Ned and Agnes Ruud sold crawlers and rented rowboats on Larch Pond for thirty-one summers. When Pell's Crossing went dark they brought their grandson Pip down from town: Ned had read in the Cordon pamphlet that water masks scent, and nothing that walks the shore would follow them out over the pond. He padlocked the shop, boarded its windows and moved the three of them into the net loft above the boat slip, and pulled the ladder up after them each night. For five weeks they fished through a hole in the loft floor. Then three mill hands came down the shore path wanting a boat, and Ned let them sleep in the boat bay. The fever went through the bay in four days, and through Ned. He went into the shop for the shells and the cash box and Agnes padlocked the bait-room door behind him. She sent Pip across the pond in the canoe to the Okafors' at first light, hung Ned's keys on the nail by the hatch and wrote it all down. The mill hands still lie among the life jackets in the bay.",
